.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt DUP 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dup ,
.Nm dup2 ,
.Nm dup3
.Nd duplicate a file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn dup "int oldfd"
.Ft int
.Fn dup2 "int oldfd" "int newfd"
.Ft int
.Fn dup3 "int oldfd" "int newfd" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn dup
duplicates
.Fa oldfd
onto the lowest unused descriptor.
The descriptors share one open-file description, including the file
offset and the
.Dv O_APPEND
and
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
status flags.
Closing one descriptor leaves the other usable.
The new descriptor's
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
flag is clear.
.Pp
.Fn dup2
duplicates
.Fa oldfd
onto
.Fa newfd .
If
.Fa newfd
is open, the kernel closes it first; close errors are ignored.
The destination is busy, and the call fails with
.Er EBUSY ,
while an
.Xr open 2
in another thread has reserved it, or while a call of another thread
sharing the descriptor table uses the descriptor there, or the one a
close left to such a call, or while a close in another thread is still
releasing it; Linux gives
.Er EBUSY
for the first.
If both arguments are equal and valid, and below the current descriptor
limit, the call returns
.Fa newfd
without changing it, including its close-on-exec flag; an existing
descriptor at or above a lowered
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
gives
.Er EBADF .
Otherwise the new descriptor's
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
flag is clear.
.Pp
.Fn dup3
performs the same replacement, but equal descriptors fail with
.Er EINVAL .
The
.Fa flags
argument must be 0 or
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
The latter sets
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
on the new descriptor.
.Pp
The descriptor table starts with 16 entries and grows to at most 1024.
The soft
.Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
limit is one greater than the highest new descriptor allowed.
For
.Fn dup2
and
.Fn dup3 ,
a negative destination or one at or above this limit gives
.Er EBADF .
.Fn dup
gives
.Er EMFILE
when no descriptor below the limit is free.
A new descriptor past the end of the table grows it, and a kernel heap
with no room for the larger table gives
.Er ENOMEM ,
as on Linux.
.Fn dup
also makes an open-file description for its new descriptor, which it
drops at once to share the old one's, and a heap with no room for that
gives
.Er ENOMEM
too; Linux's
.Fn dup
makes no description.
.Pp
The kernel releases a pipe, terminal or pseudo-terminal when the last
descriptor referring to its open-file description is closed.
Socket references are counted separately for each descriptor;
each duplicate adds a reference.
Replacing an open destination that holds the last reference to its
description also releases the framebuffer when the caller owns it.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 41 for
.Fn dup ,
63 for
.Fn dup2 ,
and 330 for
.Fn dup3 .
On x86-64 they are 32, 33 and 292.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return the new descriptor on success.
On failure they return -1 and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/dup2test.c
opens
.Pa dup2test.tmp ,
duplicates its descriptor onto standard output, writes
.Ql Hello from dup2! ,
and reads the file back to check the redirection.
.Pa tests/ofdprobe.c
checks that reads through duplicate descriptors share an offset and
that a duplicate remains usable after the original closes.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[EINVAL]"
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fa oldfd
is invalid, or
.Fa newfd
is negative or at or above the descriptor limit.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
.Fa newfd
is busy, as described above.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn dup3
was given equal descriptors or flags other than 0 or
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
.Fn dup
has no free descriptor below the limit.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel heap has no room for a larger descriptor table, or for the
open-file description
.Fn dup
makes.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr prlimit64 2
